            <ArticleTitle>Nurse Residency Programs: An integrative Review of the Literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>96-101</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5480/13-1229</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The aim is to explore nurse residency programs described in the literature and evaluate the evidence supporting their use.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The Institute of medicine and the Joint Commission recommend that organizations provide nurse residency programs for newly licensed nurses.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">An integrative review was conducted of empirical and program development articles published from 2006 to july 2013.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Most articles included in this study had limitations, which are discussed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The literature demonstrates benefits associated with NRPs. More evidence is needed to determine if NRPs contribute to nurse-sensitive patient outcomes, thereby influencing quality of nursing care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
